From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: captain <captain@lcars.local>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 19:00:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Pinefeat CEF: optional extended set for the Basilisk E-mount
 lens controller

The Basilisk board drives a Sony E-mount lens and serves the Pinefeat
protocol on `v f r e a d s c`, so this driver runs it as is. It also
serves letters a Pinefeat controller doesn't know. This change adds them
as an optional extended set, enabled only for a controller that reports
it.

At the connection, after `v`, the driver sends `t`. A Pinefeat
controller answers `er` and gets the driver as before. A controller
whose reply carries the token ext=1 gets the extended set:

- APERTURE (FNUMBER, read only): the current f-number, `o`;
- FOCUS_ABORT_MOTION (FOCUSER_CAN_ABORT): `q`;
- FOCUS_MARK and FOCUS_MARK_CTL: a focus position stored in the board,
  read by `j`, set, reached and cleared by `js`, `jg`, `jx`;
- LED_BRIGHTNESS (Options tab): the status LED, `k`, saved in the
  config and sent again at each connection;
- LENS_REBOOT: `b`;
- LENS_INFO (LENS_NAME): the lens's name, `i`;
- FOCUS_TEMPERATURE, FOCUS_HUMIDITY and FOCUS_DEW_POINT: the board's
  sensor, `z`, read once a second, while the focuser moves too, each
  defined only while the board reports its value;
- BOARD_RESTART (CAUSE, Info tab): the cause of the board's last
  restart, `x`, read with the time since it, once a second, while the
  focuser moves too. A cause that differs, or a time less than it can
  be without a restart (the last one, plus the host's time from that
  reply to this command, less 10 ms and 0.1 %), is a new restart, the
  port open or not: a crash, a watchdog, the supply or a cause the
  board doesn't know is logged as a warning, once per restart, at the
  connection too; the board resumes its lens after it, and the focus
  moves;
- the end of a move read by `g` (Ok, Idle when aborted, Alert when
  stalled or when the stop is not confirmed);
- the bounds of `r` as the range of the absolute position and of
  FOCUS_MAX, at each read, and CALIBRATE answered by them: `r` read
  again, no `c`, no Busy (the board's bounds are the lens's own,
  there is nothing to calibrate).

Without ext=1, no property of the set is defined and none of its letters
is sent, even when a client writes one of its properties by name.

Before `t` answers, the driver can't know which controller it talks to,
so two tolerances apply to every controller. A line starting with '*'
(the board's log, when its bench page left it on) is never a reply: it
is skipped up to its line end. At `v`, only a line of the form of a
version (`<major>.<minor>`, what readFirmwareVersion() reads) is the
reply: opening the port can restart the board, and the lines of its ROM
come after `v`. Both are read within the reply's deadline: a command
fails CEF_TIMEOUT after it was sent, whatever the controller keeps
sending, lines or a line without end. A Pinefeat controller sends no
reply starting with '*', and answers `v` with its version: it sees `t`,
once per connection.

One behaviour changes for any controller: a reply to `v` that is not of
the form `<major>.<minor>` was taken as the version (its minor read as
0) and the controller connected; it is now skipped as a ROM line, the
three tries of the handshake run out, and the controller is not
detected. No Pinefeat firmware is known to answer so.

The minor of `v` still decides whether `r` is read, as before.
